l inputs (no secrets hard-coded)  \\n  /dns.tf                – Route 53 records  \\n  /snapshot.tf           – Lightsail snapshot  \\n  /outputs.tf            – consolidated outputs  \\n  /README.md             – usage & cost guide  \\n\\n(The Attendant agents that will author each file will be recruited **after DEP approval**.)\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n2. Terraform Workflow (deterministic)\\n\\nStep 0  – Local prerequisites  \\n  • Terraform ≥1.5 installed  \\n  • AWS credentials configured via env vars or shared profile  \\n  • Existing public/hosted zone “myhoa.org” in Route 53  \\n  • An SSH key pair available locally (or let Terraform create a temporary one)  \\n\\nStep 1  – Provider + global settings (main.tf)  \\n  • terraform { required_providers { aws = \\\"~> 5.24\\\" } }  \\n  • provider \\\"aws\\\" { region = var.aws_region }  \\n  • resource \\\"aws_ebs_encryption_by_default\\\" \\\"this\\\" { enabled = true }   # one-time global  \\n\\nStep 2  – Lightsail WordPress instance + Static IP  \\n  • resource \\\"aws_lightsail_static_ip\\\" \\\"wp_ip\\\" { name = \\\"${var.instance_name}-ip\\\" }  \\n  • resource \\\"aws_lightsail_instance\\\" \\\"wp\\\" {  \\n        name          = var.instance_name  \\n        availability_zone = var.availability_zone  \\n        blueprint_id  = \\\"wordpress\\\"                  # WordPress-latest Bitnami image  \\n        bundle_id     = var.wp_bundle_id             # micro_2_0  ($10/mo)  \\n        key_pair_name = aws_lightsail_key_pair.kp.name  \\n        user_data     = file(\\\"scripts/run_bncert.sh\\\")   # non-interactive wrapper  \\n        tags          = merge(var.additional_tags, { \\\"Name\\\" = \\\"hoa-prod-wp\\\" })  \\n    }  \\n  • resource \\\"aws_lightsail_static_ip_attachment\\\" \\\"attach\\\" {  \\n        static_ip_name   = aws_lightsail_static_ip.wp_ip.name  \\n        instance_name    = aws_lightsail_instance.wp.name  \\n    }  \\n\\nStep 3  – BNcert HTTPS hardening (non-interactive)  \\n  • file(\\\"scripts/run_bncert.sh\\\") is rendered by a `templatefile()` to inject domain variables.  \\n    Inside, it SSHs to localhost (the instance itself) and runs:  \\n        sudo /opt/bitnami/bncert-tool --domains \\\"${var.domain_name},www.${var.domain_name}\\\" \\\\\\n             --email \\\"${var.acme_email}\\\" --agree-to-terms --redirect-to-https --noninteractive  \\n  • Executed via `user_data`, so it runs once on first boot – deterministic and idempotent.  \\n\\nStep 4  – Route 53 records (dns.tf)  \\n  • data \\\"aws_route53_zone\\\" \\\"root\\\" { name = var.domain_name }  \\n  • aws_route53_record \\\"root_a\\\" { zone_id = data.aws_route53_zone.root.zone_id ... }  \\n  • aws_route53_record \\\"www_cname\\\" { … records = [var.domain_name] }  \\n\\nStep 5  – Snapshot immediately after apply (snapshot.tf)  \\n  • resource \\\"aws_lightsail_instance_snapshot\\\" \\\"baseline\\\" {  \\n        name          = \\\"baseline-clean\\\"  \\n        instance_name = aws_lightsail_instance.wp.name  \\n        depends_on    = [aws_lightsail_static_ip_attachment.attach]  \\n    }  \\n\\nStep 6  – Outputs (outputs.tf)  \\n  • lightsail_instance_id, static_ip, snapshot_arn, root_record_fqdn, www_record_fqdn  \\n\\nStep 7  – Destruction  \\n  `terraform destroy` will remove records, snapshot, IP & instance in correct order.  \\n  (AWS forbids deleting snapshots while instance exists; TF graph handles dependency.)\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n3. Non-interactive bncert Implementation Details\\n\\nWe leverage Bitnami’s CLI flags (supported Nov 2023+) to skip the TUI.  \\nExample command (rendered in `user_data`):\\n\\n    sudo /opt/bitnami/bncert-tool \\\\\\n      --domains \\\"myhoa.org,www.myhoa.org\\\" \\\\\\n      --email \\\"admin@myhoa.org\\\" \\\\\\n      --agree-to-terms \\\\\\n      --redirect-to-https \\\\\\n      --noninteractive\\n\\nIf Bitnami ever alters flags, the script exits non-zero and Terraform shows failure – user can then SSH and run bncert manually (single contingency).\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n4. Variables & Secrets (variables.tf)\\n\\n  aws_region         default \\\"us-east-1\\\"  \\n  domain_name        default \\\"myhoa.org\\\"  \\n  instance_name      default \\\"hoa-prod-wp\\\"  \\n  wp_bundle_id       default \\\"micro_2_0\\\"  \\n  availability_zone  default \\\"us-east-1a\\\"  \\n  key_pair_name      (optional, else auto-created)  \\n  acme_email         (sensitive) – Let’s Encrypt registration  \\n  additional_tags    map(string)  \\n\\nSensitive fields marked `sensitive = true`; values passed via `TF_VAR_*` env vars\\nor `terraform.tfvars`.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n5. Cost Estimate (monthly, USD)\\n\\n  • Lightsail micro_2_0 bundle (1 GB RAM, 1 vCPU, 40 GB SSD)      $10.00  \\n  • Lightsail Static IP                                           (included)  \\n  • Route 53 Hosted Zone (already exists)                          $0.50  \\n  • Route 53 two DNS queries (negligible)                           <0.01  \\n  • Snapshot storage (40 GB) ≈ $0.12/GB-mo → ~$4.80               $4.80  \\n  ———————————————————————————————————————————  \\n  Approx. first month                                              $15.30  \\n\\nDestroying snapshot & instance brings cost to <$1/mo.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n6. Idempotency & Safety\\n\\n  • TF state tracks whether bncert has executed (via `user_data` hash).  \\n  • Lightsail blueprints create a unique password; credentials output suppressed.  \\n  • All resources tagged for easy audit & cost allocation.  \\n  • Uses global EBS default encryption – meets compliance.  \\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n7. Potential Manual Step (only if automation fails)\\n\\nIf Bitnami bncert CLI flags change, HTTPS may not be configured.  \\nFallback: SSH (`ssh ubuntu@<static-ip> -i <private_key>`) and run `sudo /opt/bitnami/bncert-tool` interactively once, then re-run `terraform apply` (which will no-op).\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n8. Next Actions\\n\\n 1. Await Director approval of this DEP.  \\n 2. Upon approval, Supervisor will recruit Attendant agents per file list above, each producing their respective artifact.  \\n 3. When all files are returned & validated, Supervisor will submit final REPORT with code artifacts zipped or inline blocks, satisfying Order infra-01-plan.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nEND OF REPORT\"}]},\"finishReason\":\"stop\",\"usage\":{\"inputTokens\":6919,\"outputTokens\":2179,\"totalTokens\":9098},\"custom\":{\"id\":\"chatcmpl-Bxee7AJkW5vFWNdaDkPPHK2XXhuRZ\",\"object\":\"chat.completion\",\"created\":1753557295,\"model\":\"o3-2025-04-16\",\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"### REPORT for Order infra-01-plan\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nDETERMINISTIC EXECUTABLE PLAN (DEP v2) – Execution Task 1  \\nObjective: Provision a hardened WordPress‐latest site on AWS Lightsail, wire DNS in Route 53, secure it with HTTPS via `bncert-tool`, and capture a clean baseline snapshot – all as reproducible IaC.\\n\\nThis DEP is written for later execution from the project root with:\\n\\n    terraform init\\n    terraform apply -auto-approve\\n    # (optional) terraform destroy -auto-approve\\n\\nNo manual console clicks are required except *one* contingency noted in §7.\\nAll Terraform resources are available in the AWS provider v5.24+.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n1. Repository / File Layout (OFAAT)\\n\\n  /